# 🐞 bug report

### Affected Package
The issue is caused by package @angular/core


### Is this a regression?

It did not work in 8.2.14 and it does not work in 9.0.3.


### Description

I have a collection `baseOrder`, which I track by `id` field and there are multiple items with that id. Then I remove the item from the index 1, then readd it in the same place, then remove, and readd, and so on. When removing the second time the `IterableDiffer` tells that, apart from just remove operation, there is also an additional operation -> position change (From index 3 to 1).

What is worth noting is that the collection order is correct - the item removed is gone and other elements didn't change place. But when listing the operations it says the item from index 3 has been moved to index 1). 

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/10242142/75114050-1999b700-5653-11ea-8f02-ba37099528bf.png)


```typescript
const _differs = iterableDiffers.find([]).create<RenderRow>((index, item) => {
  return item.data.id;
});

const baseOrder: RenderRow[] = [
  {data: {id: 'abc'}, dataIndex: 0},
  {data: {id: 'abc'}, dataIndex: 1},
  {data: {id: 'def'}, dataIndex: 2},
  {data: {id: 'def'}, dataIndex: 3},
  {data: {id: 'vwx'}, dataIndex: 4},
  {data: {id: 'xyz'}, dataIndex: 5},
];

const changedOrder: RenderRow[] = [
  {data: {id: 'abc'}, dataIndex: 0},
  {data: {id: 'def'}, dataIndex: 2},
  {data: {id: 'def'}, dataIndex: 3},
  {data: {id: 'vwx'}, dataIndex: 4},
  {data: {id: 'xyz'}, dataIndex: 5},
];

this._differs.diff(this.baseOrder);

// ---- STEP 1: I'm removing one element from index [1] ----
this._differs.diff(this.changedOrder);
this._differs.forEachOperation(console.log);
// 1 change: index 1 -> null

// ---- STEP 2: I'm readding the same element to index [1] ----
this._differs.diff(this.baseOrder);
this._differs.forEachOperation(console.log);
// 1 change: index null => 1

// ---- STEP 3: I'm removing that element from index [1] again (Should be the same as in STEP 1) ----
this._differs.diff(this.changedOrder);
this._differs.forEachOperation(console.log);
// 2 changes: index 1 -> null, index 3 -> 1

// !!! The differs' internal collection reflects the order CORRECTLY, but the operations, which Angular CDK  Table uses internally tells something else.
```


